1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,790
Conductor runs a team of coding agents on any codebase.

2
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:06,430
On one of mine: 592 tasks, 197 agent hours,

3
00:00:06,430 --> 00:00:08,070
and 404 commits on main.

4
00:00:08,422 --> 00:00:08,962
Opus stays the manager.

5
00:00:09,662 --> 00:00:12,062
Over MCP it spawns up to eight opencode agents on

6
00:00:12,062 --> 00:00:14,042
free models like Space Bunny and GLM,

7
00:00:14,042 --> 00:00:16,198
so the grunt work never spends Opus usage.

8
00:00:16,198 --> 00:00:17,718
Each one gets a role, its own git worktree,

9
00:00:17,718 --> 00:00:19,298
and a locked list of files.

10
00:00:19,889 --> 00:00:21,309
Then Opus waits on one shell command that

11
00:00:21,389 --> 00:00:22,149
returns the moment they finish,

12
00:00:22,649 --> 00:00:24,169
so its context stays clean.

13
00:00:24,808 --> 00:00:26,328
Those agents went through about 20 billion

14
00:00:26,328 --> 00:00:28,128
tokens and over 45,000 tool calls,

15
00:00:28,128 --> 00:00:29,690
none of it spent on Opus.

16
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:31,910
Every prompt puts its stable part first: the role,

17
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:33,870
the skills and the repo map.

18
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:36,890
So 95 to 99.97 percent of input came straight from cache.

19
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:39,410
It's a deliberate balance, tuned for security and speed:

20
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:42,302
most work runs on its own, between Claude and Conductor.

21
00:00:42,302 --> 00:00:44,442
I step in only for a gate, a paid model or a stuck task,

22
00:00:44,442 --> 00:00:46,882
and I can stop or steer any run at any time.

23
00:00:47,091 --> 00:00:48,931
When a run finishes, Claude checks and verifies it,

24
00:00:49,251 --> 00:00:50,658
then merges it or flags it for me.

25
00:00:50,658 --> 00:00:51,658
Rate limits skip to the next provider,

26
00:00:52,238 --> 00:00:53,758
and it's all one Python process.

